%[text] %[text:anchor:H_63FC2BC8] ### Define the closed-loop transfer function
%[text] With the transfer function of the plant identified, the closed-loop transfer function can be generated. You can derive this from the block diagram.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![piddiagramblocks.png](text:image:8bc8)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Block diagram for the PID control feedback loop. The closed-loop transfer function* $C(s)$*encapsulates the entire feedback loop. The reference value denoted* $R(s)$ *is the input of* $C$ *and its output is* $Y(s)$*.* 
%[text] The closed-loop transfer function $C$ can be easily derived from the block diagram to be
%[text]{"align":"center"} $C = \\frac{Y}{R} = \\frac{DG}{1+DG}$
%[text] The plant transfer function $G$ was identified in the previous section. The controller transfer function $D$ must also be known. A PID controller computes the control signal as
%[text]{"align":"center"} $u(t) = k\_p e + k\_i \\int\_0^\\tau e(\\tau) d \\tau + k\_d \\frac{de}{dt}$
%[text] You can compute the controller transfer function $D$ by taking the Laplace transform and then solving for the ratio of the input error, $E(s)$, to the output control signal, $U(s)$:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $D(s) = \\frac{U(s)}{E(s)} = k\_p + \\frac{k\_i}{s} + k\_d s$
%%
