%[text] %[text:anchor:H_17680EF6] ## Stability of the feedback controller
%[text] The **poles** of a rational transfer function occur where the denominator is zero. Consider a transfer function in factored form:
%[text]  $G(s) = K \\frac{ (s-z\_1)(s-z\_2) \\ldots (s-z\_m) }{ (s-p\_1)(s-p\_2) \\ldots (s-p\_n)}$
%[text] The poles are $p\_1$, $p\_2$, $\\ldots$, $p\_n$. Recall that the poles of a transfer function determine its general behavior and stability.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![polePlaneV4.png](text:image:6d5d)
%[text]{"align":"center"}  *Impulse responses characterized by the position of poles in the complex* $s$*-plane. Poles in the left-half plane (where* $\\text{Re}(p) \< 0$*) are stable.*
%[text] If all the poles of a transfer function lie in the left-half plane, then the transfer function is stable. In this section, you will assess the stability of the feedback controller by examining the poles of its closed-loop transfer function.
%%
